  "content": "Here is a comprehensive guide to solving quadratic equations using the quadratic formula, including its derivation, analysis of the discriminant, and detailed worked examples.\n\n### 1. General Form of a Quadratic Equation\n\nA standard quadratic equation is written in the form:\n$$ ax^2 + bx + c = 0 $$\nwhere $a$, $b$, and $c$ are real numbers, and $a \\neq 0$. If $a=0$, the equation becomes linear.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Derivation of the Quadratic Formula (Completing the Square)\n\nWe will derive the formula by applying the method of completing the square to the general equation.\n\n**Step 1:** Start with the general equation.\n$$ ax^2 + bx + c = 0 $$\n\n**Step 2:** Move the constant term $c$ to the right side.\n$$ ax^2 + bx = -c $$\n\n**Step 3:** Divide every term by $a$ to make the coefficient of $x^2$ equal to 1.\n$$ x^2 + \\frac{b}{a}x = -\\frac{c}{a} $$\n\n**Step 4:** Complete the square.",
